/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Ecclesiastes chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ecclesiastes", [
{n:6, name:"The Man Who Could Not Eat",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:9",verse:"Better is the sight of the eyes than the wandering of the desire: this is also vanity and vexation of spirit."},
 facts:["Did you know the Preacher pictures a man given riches, wealth, and honour, everything he desires, yet denied even the power to eat of it, while a stranger eats it? He calls this 'an evil disease' (Ecclesiastes 6:2).","Did you know the Preacher says that even a man who begets a hundred children and lives many years, but whose soul is not filled with good and who has no burial, is worse off than an untimely birth (Ecclesiastes 6:3)?","Did you know the chapter's quiet cure for restless desire is to look at what is in front of you? 'Better is the sight of the eyes than the wandering of the desire' (Ecclesiastes 6:9)."],
 questions:[
{q:"What kind of thing does the Preacher say he has seen under the sun?", options:["An evil, and it is common among men","A wonder, rarely seen","A blessing, hidden from men","A mystery, known only to kings"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:1",verse:"There is an evil which I have seen under the sun, and it is common among men:", insight:"The Preacher does not hide life's hard cases; faith looks honestly at what is common before asking God for wisdom."},
{q:"Where did the Preacher see this evil?", options:["Under the sun","Beyond the sea","In the king's court","In a distant land"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:1",verse:"There is an evil which I have seen under the sun, and it is common among men:", insight:"'Under the sun' is his phrase for life as we all live it; his hard questions are about your world, not some other world."},
{q:"What three things had God given the man in verse 2?", options:["Riches, wealth, and honour","Wisdom, strength, and years","Sons, daughters, and land","Flocks, herds, and silver"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:2",verse:"A man to whom God hath given riches, wealth, and honour, so that he wanteth nothing for his soul of all that he desireth, yet God giveth him not power to eat thereof, but a stranger eateth it: this is vanity, and it is an evil disease.", insight:"He wanted nothing for his soul of all he desired; the heart can own everything and still go hungry."},
{q:"What power did God not give that rich man?", options:["Power to eat of his riches","Power to count his wealth","Power to leave it to his sons","Power to build with it"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:2",verse:"A man to whom God hath given riches, wealth, and honour, so that he wanteth nothing for his soul of all that he desireth, yet God giveth him not power to eat thereof, but a stranger eateth it: this is vanity, and it is an evil disease.", insight:"Having and enjoying are two different gifts; only God grants the second, so thank Him for your appetite, not just your table."},
{q:"How many children did the second man beget?", options:["An hundred","Seven","Twelve","Seventy"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:3",verse:"If a man beget an hundred children, and live many years, so that the days of his years be many, and his soul be not filled with good, and also that he have no burial; I say, that an untimely birth is better than he.", insight:"A hundred children and many years: the Preacher stacks up every human measure of a full life, then weighs the soul."},
{q:"What did the Preacher say was better than that man?", options:["An untimely birth","A poor man's child","A servant's grave","A stranger's life"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:3",verse:"If a man beget an hundred children, and live many years, so that the days of his years be many, and his soul be not filled with good, and also that he have no burial; I say, that an untimely birth is better than he.", insight:"A shocking verdict meant to shake us: length of life is nothing if the soul never lives. Do not merely last; truly live."},
{q:"How does the untimely birth come and depart?", options:["It comes in with vanity, and departs in darkness","It comes with joy, and departs with sorrow","It comes in light, and departs in peace","It comes in silence, and departs with honour"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:4",verse:"For he cometh in with vanity, and departeth in darkness, and his name shall be covered with darkness.", insight:"A life that begins and ends in shadow sounds bleak, and that is the point: without God, even a long life differs little from none."},
{q:"What had the untimely birth never done?", options:["Seen the sun, nor known any thing","Eaten bread, nor drunk wine","Known his parents, nor his name","Worked in the field, nor rested"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:5",verse:"Moreover he hath not seen the sun, nor known any thing: this hath more rest than the other.", insight:"Never seeing the sun, it rests more than the man who saw everything and tasted nothing. Enjoyment is a mercy; ask God for it."},
{q:"Who had more rest, the rich man or the untimely birth?", options:["The untimely birth","The rich man","Neither of them","Both equally"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:5",verse:"Moreover he hath not seen the sun, nor known any thing: this hath more rest than the other.", insight:"Rest belongs to the one who never laboured for what he could not keep; busyness is no proof of life."},
{q:"How long might the man live, yet still see no good?", options:["A thousand years twice told","An hundred years","Seven hundred years","Fifty years twice told"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:6",verse:"Yea, though he live a thousand years twice told, yet hath he seen no good: do not all go to one place?", insight:"Two thousand years of breathing would still be vanity without seeing good; years add nothing to a joyless soul."},
{q:"Where do both the long lived and the short lived go?", options:["All go to one place","The wise to one place, the fool to another","The rich above, the poor below","No one can tell"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:6",verse:"Yea, though he live a thousand years twice told, yet hath he seen no good: do not all go to one place?", insight:"One place: the grave levels every life. Remembering this keeps us humble and urgent about the things that outlast it."},
{q:"What is all the labour of man for?", options:["His mouth","His house","His children","His king"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:7",verse:"All the labour of man is for his mouth, and yet the appetite is not filled.", insight:"All our work feeds the mouth, the Preacher says; honest labour is good, but labour aimed only at appetite never ends."},
{q:"What never happens to the appetite?", options:["It is not filled","It grows weaker with age","It turns from food to wisdom","It is satisfied at last"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:7",verse:"All the labour of man is for his mouth, and yet the appetite is not filled.", insight:"The appetite is never filled. Chasing satisfaction in appetite alone is drinking salt water to quench thirst."},
{q:"What advantage has the wise over the fool, according to verse 8?", options:["Nothing stated; he asks what more the wise has than the fool","Great riches","A longer life","Many servants"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:8",verse:"For what hath the wise more than the fool? what hath the poor, that knoweth to walk before the living?", insight:"The Preacher asks, not answers: what has the wise more than the fool? Wisdom must point beyond itself to God, or it too is empty."},
{q:"What does the poor man know how to do?", options:["Walk before the living","Gather riches in secret","Rule over his household","Read the stars"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:8",verse:"For what hath the wise more than the fool? what hath the poor, that knoweth to walk before the living?", insight:"The poor man who knows how to walk before the living has learned what the rich man missed: how to conduct himself before God and men."},
{q:"What is better than the wandering of the desire?", options:["The sight of the eyes","The dreams of the night","The counsel of many","The riches of the king"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:9",verse:"Better is the sight of the eyes than the wandering of the desire: this is also vanity and vexation of spirit.", insight:"Look at what your eyes can see instead of chasing what desire imagines. Contentment starts with noticing today's mercies."},
{q:"With whom may man not contend?", options:["Him that is mightier than he","His neighbour","His servant","The stranger"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:10",verse:"That which hath been is named already, and it is known that it is man: neither may he contend with him that is mightier than he.", insight:"Man is named already, known to be man; contending with the Mightier One is folly. Yield to God, and stop wrestling the Almighty."},
{q:"Seeing there be many things that increase vanity, what is man the better?", options:["Nothing is said to make him better; the question stands unanswered","He is the better for his wisdom","He is the better for his wealth","He is the better for his years"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:11",verse:"Seeing there be many things that increase vanity, what is man the better?", insight:"Many things increase vanity, and the Preacher asks what man gains; the silence is the answer. Only God adds what truly betters us."},
{q:"What does no man know about this life?", options:["What is good for man in it","How long it will last","Who his friends are","Where it began"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:12",verse:"For who knoweth what is good for man in this life, all the days of his vain life which he spendeth as a shadow? for who can tell a man what shall be after him under the sun?", insight:"Who knows what is good for us in this life? We do not, but God does; trust His wisdom where your own runs out."},
{q:"How does man spend the days of his vain life?", options:["As a shadow","As a king","As a song","As a feast"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 6:12",verse:"For who knoweth what is good for man in this life, all the days of his vain life which he spendeth as a shadow? for who can tell a man what shall be after him under the sun?", insight:"Our days pass as a shadow, brief and ungraspable; shadows cannot be held, so hold the One who outlasts them."}
]},
{n:7, name:"The Better Things",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:29",verse:"Lo, this only have I found, that God hath made man upright; but they have sought out many inventions."},
 facts:["Did you know this chapter holds one of Scripture's most quoted proverbs, that 'a good name is better than precious ointment'? Ointment was costly and fragrant in his world, yet reputation outweighed it (Ecclesiastes 7:1).","Did you know the Preacher compares a fool's laughter to the crackling of thorns under a pot? Thorn fires blaze up fast and loud, then die out and leave nothing (Ecclesiastes 7:6).","Did you know that after counting people one by one in his search, the Preacher's conclusion was not despair but this: 'God hath made man upright; but they have sought out many inventions' (Ecclesiastes 7:27-29)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What is better than precious ointment?", options:["A good name","A full purse","A fine garment","A strong house"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:1",verse:"A good name is better than precious ointment; and the day of death than the day of one’s birth.", insight:"Ointment perfumes the body for a day; a good name perfumes your memory for years. Guard your character like treasure."},
{q:"What is better than the day of one's birth?", options:["The day of death","The day of marriage","The day of wealth","The day of honour"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:1",verse:"A good name is better than precious ointment; and the day of death than the day of one’s birth.", insight:"Death day better than birthday? The Preacher means a finished life, faithfully lived, outweighs a promising start. Finish well."},
{q:"Which house is better to visit than the house of feasting?", options:["The house of mourning","The house of the king","The house of merchandise","The house of music"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:2",verse:"It is better to go to the house of mourning, than to go to the house of feasting: for that is the end of all men; and the living will lay it to his heart.", insight:"Mourning teaches what feasting never will: life is short. The wise let grief do its deep work in them."},
{q:"What is better than laughter?", options:["Sorrow","Sleep","Silence","Song"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:3",verse:"Sorrow is better than laughter: for by the sadness of the countenance the heart is made better.", insight:"By the sadness of the countenance the heart is made better. God uses sorrow as a school; do not rush past its lessons."},
{q:"Where is the heart of the wise?", options:["In the house of mourning","In the house of feasting","In the king's court","In the marketplace"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:4",verse:"The heart of the wise is in the house of mourning; but the heart of fools is in the house of mirth.", insight:"The wise keep company with life's serious truths, while fools chase the next laugh. Choose your rooms carefully."},
{q:"What is better than hearing the song of fools?", options:["Hearing the rebuke of the wise","Hearing the praise of friends","Hearing the music of the temple","Hearing the tales of travellers"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:5",verse:"It is better to hear the rebuke of the wise, than for a man to hear the song of fools.", insight:"Rebuke stings and heals; a fool's song soothes and empties. Welcome the friend who tells you the hard truth."},
{q:"What is the laughter of the fool like?", options:["The crackling of thorns under a pot","The roaring of a lion","The singing of birds","The rushing of waters"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:6",verse:"For as the crackling of thorns under a pot, so is the laughter of the fool: this also is vanity.", insight:"Thorns blaze loud and brief under a pot, then leave only ash. Foolish laughter is noise without warmth; do not build your joy on it."},
{q:"What does a gift do to the heart?", options:["It destroyeth the heart","It strengtheneth the heart","It cleanseth the heart","It gladdeneth the heart"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:7",verse:"Surely oppression maketh a wise man mad; and a gift destroyeth the heart.", insight:"A bribe corrupts judgment, even in a wise man. Keep your hands clean, and your decisions will stay clear."},
{q:"What is better than the beginning of a thing?", options:["The end thereof","The middle thereof","The promise thereof","The planning thereof"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:8",verse:"Better is the end of a thing than the beginning thereof: and the patient in spirit is better than the proud in spirit.", insight:"Beginnings are hopeful; endings reveal what we became. Judge a work, and a life, by how it finishes."},
{q:"Who is better than the proud in spirit?", options:["The patient in spirit","The rich in goods","The swift in battle","The merry in heart"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:8",verse:"Better is the end of a thing than the beginning thereof: and the patient in spirit is better than the proud in spirit.", insight:"Patience outlasts pride. The proud spirit breaks; the patient spirit endures and wins."},
{q:"Where does anger rest?", options:["In the bosom of fools","In the house of kings","In the fields of war","In the depths of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:9",verse:"Be not hasty in thy spirit to be angry: for anger resteth in the bosom of fools.", insight:"Anger makes its home in fools. Be slow to anger, and you prove yourself no fool's lodging."},
{q:"What should you not say about former days?", options:["What is the cause that the former days were better than these?","Where have all the wise men gone?","Who will lead us as before?","How long will these days last?"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:10",verse:"Say not thou, What is the cause that the former days were better than these? for thou dost not enquire wisely concerning this.", insight:"Nostalgia is not wisdom. God is at work in these days too; serve Him in the present instead of sighing for the past."},
{q:"What two things are called a defence?", options:["Wisdom and money","Strength and speed","Friends and family","Gold and silver"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:12",verse:"For wisdom is a defence, and money is a defence: but the excellency of knowledge is, that wisdom giveth life to them that have it.", insight:"Both wisdom and money shelter us, the Preacher admits; but only wisdom gives life to those who have it. Seek the living defence."},
{q:"Who can make straight what God has made crooked?", options:["No man can","The wise man","The king","The strong man"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:13",verse:"Consider the work of God: for who can make that straight, which he hath made crooked?", insight:"Some bends in life are God's own doing. Stop fighting His shaping and start trusting His hand."},
{q:"What should you do in the day of prosperity, and in the day of adversity?", options:["Be joyful in prosperity, and consider in adversity","Feast in prosperity, and weep in adversity","Share in prosperity, and hide in adversity","Sing in prosperity, and sleep in adversity"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:14",verse:"In the day of prosperity be joyful, but in the day of adversity consider: God also hath set the one over against the other, to the end that man should find nothing after him.", insight:"God set prosperity and adversity side by side, so we would hold both loosely and Him tightly. Joy and reflection each have their day."},
{q:"What strange things had the Preacher seen in the days of his vanity?", options:["A just man perishing in his righteousness, and a wicked man prolonging his life in wickedness","A king serving his people, and a servant ruling his master","The sun standing still, and the moon delaying","Rivers running uphill, and stones floating"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:15",verse:"All things have I seen in the days of my vanity: there is a just man that perisheth in his righteousness, and there is a wicked man that prolongeth his life in his wickedness.", insight:"Life under the sun does not always reward the righteous promptly. Trust God's justice beyond what your eyes can yet see."},
{q:"What is said of him that feareth God?", options:["He shall come forth of them all","He shall never see trouble","He shall rule over kings","He shall live without labour"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:18",verse:"It is good that thou shouldest take hold of this; yea, also from this withdraw not thine hand: for he that feareth God shall come forth of them all.", insight:"Hold both truths, avoid both extremes, and the fear of God will bring you through them all. Reverence steadies the walk."},
{q:"What does wisdom strengthen the wise more than?", options:["Ten mighty men in the city","An hundred chariots","A wall of brass","The king's own guard"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:19",verse:"Wisdom strengtheneth the wise more than ten mighty men which are in the city.", insight:"One wise heart outweighs ten strong arms. In every crisis, godly wisdom is the greater force."},
{q:"What is true of every just man upon earth?", options:["He doeth good, and sinneth not is false of him; there is none who does good and never sins","He never suffers","He is known to all","He needs no teacher"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:20",verse:"For there is not a just man upon earth, that doeth good, and sinneth not.", insight:"There is not a just man upon earth who does good and never sins. Knowing this keeps us humble toward others and honest before God."},
{q:"What did the Preacher find more bitter than death?", options:["The woman whose heart is snares and nets, and her hands as bands","The loss of riches","The betrayal of a friend","The anger of a king"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 7:26",verse:"And I find more bitter than death the woman, whose heart is snares and nets, and her hands as bands: whoso pleaseth God shall escape from her; but the sinner shall be taken by her.", insight:"The Preacher warns of the seductress whose heart is a trap; whoever pleases God escapes her. Purity is protection, and prayer is the way out."}
]},
{n:8, name:"Wisdom Before the King",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:17",verse:"Then I beheld all the work of God, that a man cannot find out the work that is done under the sun: because though a man labour to seek it out, yet he shall not find it; yea further; though a wise man think to know it, yet shall he not be able to find it."},
 facts:["Did you know the Preacher says wisdom changes the face? 'A man's wisdom maketh his face to shine, and the boldness of his face shall be changed' (Ecclesiastes 8:1). Inner light shows on the outside.","Did you know the Preacher gives a sobering reason for rising wickedness: 'Because sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily, therefore the heart of the sons of men is fully set in them to do evil' (Ecclesiastes 8:11)?","Did you know the Preacher insists no one can retain his spirit or command the day of death, and 'there is no discharge in that war'? Death grants no exemptions and no resignations (Ecclesiastes 8:8)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Who knows the interpretation of a thing?", options:["The wise man","The king","The priest","The rich man"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:1",verse:"Who is as the wise man? and who knoweth the interpretation of a thing? a man’s wisdom maketh his face to shine, and the boldness of his face shall be changed.", insight:"Wisdom is the power to interpret life, not merely endure it. Ask God for understanding, and events begin to speak."},
{q:"What does a man's wisdom do to his face?", options:["It maketh his face to shine","It maketh his face pale","It hideth his face","It hardeneth his face"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:1",verse:"Who is as the wise man? and who knoweth the interpretation of a thing? a man’s wisdom maketh his face to shine, and the boldness of his face shall be changed.", insight:"Wisdom brightens the countenance; a heart at peace with God shows in the face. Inner light leaks outward."},
{q:"What does the Preacher counsel about the king's commandment?", options:["Keep it, in regard of the oath of God","Question it before the people","Delay it until the morrow","Write it on the gates"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:2",verse:"I counsel thee to keep the king’s commandment, and that in regard of the oath of God.", insight:"Obedience to authority is tied to our oath before God. Honour rulers because you honour Him."},
{q:"What should you not be hasty to do regarding the king?", options:["Go out of his sight","Enter into his presence","Speak in his court","Eat at his table"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:3",verse:"Be not hasty to go out of his sight: stand not in an evil thing; for he doeth whatsoever pleaseth him.", insight:"Do not storm out of the king's presence in a huff; hasty exits from authority rarely end well. Stay, and stay respectful."},
{q:"Where the word of a king is, what is there?", options:["Power","Wealth","Wisdom","Mercy"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:4",verse:"Where the word of a king is, there is power: and who may say unto him, What doest thou?", insight:"A king's word carries power; how much more the Word of the King of kings. Tremble at God's word, not man's."},
{q:"What shall the keeper of the commandment feel?", options:["No evil thing","No hunger","No sorrow","No weariness"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:5",verse:"Whoso keepeth the commandment shall feel no evil thing: and a wise man’s heart discerneth both time and judgment.", insight:"Obedience is its own shelter; the one who keeps the commandment is spared evils that swallow the defiant."},
{q:"What does a wise man's heart discern?", options:["Both time and judgment","The thoughts of kings","The secrets of the deep","The number of the stars"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:5",verse:"Whoso keepeth the commandment shall feel no evil thing: and a wise man’s heart discerneth both time and judgment.", insight:"Wisdom knows not only what is right but when; discernment of time and judgment saves us from right words at wrong hours."},
{q:"Why is the misery of man great upon him?", options:["Because to every purpose there is time and judgment, which he cannot master","Because he has no friends","Because the king is cruel","Because the harvest fails"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:6",verse:"Because to every purpose there is time and judgment, therefore the misery of man is great upon him.", insight:"Every purpose has its time, and man cannot command either; our misery grows from grasping at control God never gave us."},
{q:"What power does no man have over the spirit?", options:["Power to retain the spirit","Power to see the spirit","Power to command the spirit","Power to hide the spirit"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:8",verse:"There is no man that hath power over the spirit to retain the spirit; neither hath he power in the day of death: and there is no discharge in that war; neither shall wickedness deliver those that are given to it.", insight:"You cannot hold your breath of life by willpower; life is a loan from God. Live each day as a steward, not an owner."},
{q:"What is there no discharge from?", options:["That war, the day of death","The king's army","The yearly tribute","The winter's cold"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:8",verse:"There is no man that hath power over the spirit to retain the spirit; neither hath he power in the day of death: and there is no discharge in that war; neither shall wickedness deliver those that are given to it.", insight:"Death grants no exemptions and no resignations. Since the appointment is sure, be ready for it."},
{q:"What did the Preacher see about one man ruling over another?", options:["There is a time when it is to his own hurt","It always brings peace","It pleases God","It lasts forever"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:9",verse:"All this have I seen, and applied my heart unto every work that is done under the sun: there is a time wherein one man ruleth over another to his own hurt.", insight:"Power used to crush others turns back on the crusher; authority is a trust, and God audits every ruler."},
{q:"What happened to the wicked who came and went from the place of the holy?", options:["They were buried, and forgotten in the city where they had done so","They were honoured with monuments","They were made priests","They lived to old age in peace"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:10",verse:"And so I saw the wicked buried, who had come and gone from the place of the holy, and they were forgotten in the city where they had so done: this is also vanity.", insight:"The wicked strutted through holy places and were soon forgotten; pomp without righteousness leaves no lasting name."},
{q:"Why is the heart of the sons of men fully set to do evil?", options:["Because sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily","Because they are taught by their fathers","Because the king commands it","Because they know no law"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:11",verse:"Because sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily, therefore the heart of the sons of men is fully set in them to do evil.", insight:"Delayed judgment emboldens sinners, but delay is mercy, not approval. Do not mistake God's patience for His permission."},
{q:"Even if a sinner does evil a hundred times and his days are prolonged, what does the Preacher surely know?", options:["It shall be well with them that fear God","He will be punished tomorrow","The king will forgive him","His wealth will save him"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:12",verse:"Though a sinner do evil an hundred times, and his days be prolonged, yet surely I know that it shall be well with them that fear God, which fear before him:", insight:"The wicked may run up a long count of sins, but the Preacher is sure: it will be well with those who fear God. Fear Him, not the odds."},
{q:"How long are the days of the wicked?", options:["As a shadow, not prolonged","As the mountains, enduring","As the sea, without end","As the stars, unnumbered"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:13",verse:"But it shall not be well with the wicked, neither shall he prolong his days, which are as a shadow; because he feareth not before God.", insight:"The wicked's days are as a shadow: long-seeming, substance-less, soon gone. Build on what casts a real shadow."},
{q:"What vanity is done upon the earth?", options:["Just men suffer as the wicked deserve, and wicked men prosper as the righteous deserve","The sun rises on time","The poor are fed","The king grows old"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:14",verse:"There is a vanity which is done upon the earth; that there be just men, unto whom it happeneth according to the work of the wicked; again, there be wicked men, to whom it happeneth according to the work of the righteous: I said that this also is vanity.", insight:"The Preacher names the puzzle honestly: life's ledger looks unbalanced. Faith trusts the final audit to God."},
{q:"What did the Preacher commend, since man has no better thing under the sun?", options:["Mirth: to eat, and to drink, and to be merry","Sleep without dreams","Silence without words","Travel to far lands"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:15",verse:"Then I commended mirth, because a man hath no better thing under the sun, than to eat, and to drink, and to be merry: for that shall abide with him of his labour the days of his life, which God giveth him under the sun.", insight:"Receiving God's gifts with joy is not compromise; it is worship. Eat, drink, and be merry as one who knows the Giver."},
{q:"What will abide with a man of his labour?", options:["That mirth, all the days of his life which God giveth him","His gold and silver","His houses and lands","The praise of men"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:15",verse:"Then I commended mirth, because a man hath no better thing under the sun, than to eat, and to drink, and to be merry: for that shall abide with him of his labour the days of his life, which God giveth him under the sun.", insight:"Simple joy in daily work abides with us through life; it is God's own wage for labour, paid daily."},
{q:"What did the man see who neither day nor night saw sleep with his eyes?", options:["The business done upon the earth, and that man cannot find out God's work","The face of an angel","The end of the world","The treasures of kings"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:16",verse:"When I applied mine heart to know wisdom, and to see the business that is done upon the earth: (for also there is that neither day nor night seeth sleep with his eyes:)", insight:"Sleepless study of earth's business taught him his limits; some who chase understanding lose their rest and still miss the point."},
{q:"Even if a wise man thinks to know God's work, what is true?", options:["He shall not be able to find it out","He shall teach it to kings","He shall write it in a book","He shall be praised by all"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 8:17",verse:"Then I beheld all the work of God, that a man cannot find out the work that is done under the sun: because though a man labour to seek it out, yet he shall not find it; yea further; though a wise man think to know it, yet shall he not be able to find it.", insight:"God's work outruns our finding out; humility before mystery is wisdom's final lesson. Worship what you cannot fully explain."}
]},
{n:9, name:"One Event to All",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:10",verse:"Whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might; for there is no work, nor device, nor knowledge, nor wisdom, in the grave, whither thou goest."},
 facts:["Did you know the Preacher's blunt proverb, 'a living dog is better than a dead lion'? A dog was despised and a lion honoured in his world, yet life itself outweighs lost glory (Ecclesiastes 9:4).","Did you know the little city saved by a poor wise man was forgotten anyway? He delivered it by wisdom, 'yet no man remembered that same poor man' (Ecclesiastes 9:14-15).","Did you know the Preacher observed that 'the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong,' but 'time and chance happeneth to them all'? Strength and skill do not control outcomes (Ecclesiastes 9:11)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Where are the righteous, and the wise, and their works?", options:["In the hand of God","In the books of kings","In the memory of men","In the treasuries of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:1",verse:"For all this I considered in my heart even to declare all this, that the righteous, and the wise, and their works, are in the hand of God: no man knoweth either love or hatred by all that is before them.", insight:"Whatever others think, your life and labour rest in God's hand; that is the safest place anything can be."},
{q:"What does no man know by all that is before him?", options:["Either love or hatred","The day of his death","The name of God","The end of the world"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:1",verse:"For all this I considered in my heart even to declare all this, that the righteous, and the wise, and their works, are in the hand of God: no man knoweth either love or hatred by all that is before them.", insight:"We cannot read God's love or displeasure from outward events; prosperity is no proof of favour, nor trouble of wrath."},
{q:"What comes alike to all?", options:["One event: to the righteous and the wicked alike","One blessing","One curse","One reward"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:2",verse:"All things come alike to all: there is one event to the righteous, and to the wicked; to the good and to the clean, and to the unclean; to him that sacrificeth, and to him that sacrificeth not: as is the good, so is the sinner; and he that sweareth, as he that feareth an oath.", insight:"The same event, death, comes to all; it is the great leveller. Live today in light of the day all must face."},
{q:"How does the Preacher pair the good with the sinner, and the oath keeper with the swearer?", options:["As is the good, so is the sinner; as he that sweareth, so is he that feareth an oath","The good prospers, the sinner fails","The swearer is cursed, the oath keeper blessed","They never meet"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:2",verse:"All things come alike to all: there is one event to the righteous, and to the wicked; to the good and to the clean, and to the unclean; to him that sacrificeth, and to him that sacrificeth not: as is the good, so is the sinner; and he that sweareth, as he that feareth an oath.", insight:"Under the sun the pairs look identical; the difference is hidden with God. Do right for His sake, not for visible reward."},
{q:"What fills the heart of the sons of men while they live?", options:["Evil, and madness is in their heart","Wisdom and understanding","Peace and quietness","Joy and gladness"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:3",verse:"This is an evil among all things that are done under the sun, that there is one event unto all: yea, also the heart of the sons of men is full of evil, and madness is in their heart while they live, and after that they go to the dead.", insight:"The Preacher diagnoses the human heart honestly: full of evil and madness. Only grace, not nature, changes it."},
{q:"What is better than a dead lion?", options:["A living dog","A living lamb","A living sparrow","A living servant"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:4",verse:"For to him that is joined to all the living there is hope: for a living dog is better than a dead lion.", insight:"A despised dog alive outranks a noble lion dead; while you live, hope and opportunity live with you. Do not waste the gift of today."},
{q:"What do the living know that the dead do not?", options:["The living know that they shall die; the dead know not any thing","The living know riches; the dead know poverty","The living know joy; the dead know sorrow","The living know God; the dead know nothing of Him"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:5",verse:"For the living know that they shall die: but the dead know not any thing, neither have they any more a reward; for the memory of them is forgotten.", insight:"Knowing you will die is wisdom's beginning; it teaches you to number your days and spend them well."},
{q:"What has perished for the dead?", options:["Their love, and their hatred, and their envy","Their houses and lands","Their names and families","Their wisdom and strength"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:6",verse:"Also their love, and their hatred, and their envy, is now perished; neither have they any more a portion for ever in any thing that is done under the sun.", insight:"Even strong passions perish at death; hold love loosely enough to give it freely now, and let grudges die before you do."},
{q:"How should a man eat his bread and drink his wine?", options:["With joy and a merry heart, for God now accepteth his works","In silence and alone","Quickly, before trouble comes","Sparingly, in fear"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:7",verse:"Go thy way, eat thy bread with joy, and drink thy wine with a merry heart; for God now accepteth thy works.", insight:"God accepts your work, so receive your bread with joy; gratitude turns an ordinary meal into worship."},
{q:"What should be always white, and never lacking?", options:["Garments white, and the head lacking no ointment","The teeth, and the hands clean","The walls of the house","The pages of the book"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:8",verse:"Let thy garments be always white; and let thy head lack no ointment.", insight:"White garments and fresh ointment picture a life kept clean and glad; keep your conscience bright and your spirit fresh."},
{q:"With whom should a man live joyfully all his days?", options:["The wife whom he lovest","His brethren in the gate","His servants in the field","The elders of the city"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:9",verse:"Live joyfully with the wife whom thou lovest all the days of the life of thy vanity, which he hath given thee under the sun, all the days of thy vanity: for that is thy portion in this life, and in thy labour which thou takest under the sun.", insight:"A joyful marriage is called your portion in this life; cherish your wife as God's own gift, not as background to your labour."},
{q:"How should you do whatsoever your hand finds to do?", options:["Do it with thy might","Do it slowly and carefully","Do it only if commanded","Leave it for another"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:10",verse:"Whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might; for there is no work, nor device, nor knowledge, nor wisdom, in the grave, whither thou goest.", insight:"There is no work in the grave, so work now with all your might; wholehearted labour is the wise use of borrowed time."},
{q:"To whom is the race, and to whom the battle?", options:["The race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong","The race to the swift, the battle to the strong","The race to the young, the battle to the old","None run, none fight"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:11",verse:"I returned, and saw under the sun, that the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of understanding, nor yet favour to men of skill; but time and chance happeneth to them all.", insight:"The fastest do not always win; outcomes rest with God. Run your race faithfully and leave the finish to Him."},
{q:"What happens to them all, swift and strong, wise and skilful?", options:["Time and chance happeneth to them all","They all grow rich","They all are honoured","They all live long"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:11",verse:"I returned, and saw under the sun, that the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of understanding, nor yet favour to men of skill; but time and chance happeneth to them all.", insight:"Time and chance touch every life; this should humble the proud and comfort the struggling. God orders what looks random."},
{q:"How are the sons of men snared in an evil time?", options:["As fishes taken in an evil net, and as birds caught in the snare","As sheep led to pasture","As oxen yoked to the plough","As children called to supper"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:12",verse:"For man also knoweth not his time: as the fishes that are taken in an evil net, and as the birds that are caught in the snare; so are the sons of men snared in an evil time, when it falleth suddenly upon them.", insight:"Trouble falls suddenly, like a net over fish; since you do not know your time, walk ready every day."},
{q:"What did a great king do to the little city with few men?", options:["Besieged it, and built great bulwarks against it","Burned it with fire","Bribed its rulers","Left it in peace"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:14",verse:"There was a little city, and few men within it; and there came a great king against it, and besieged it, and built great bulwarks against it:", insight:"Great power besieged a small city; the odds looked hopeless. Remember this when your own troubles look oversized."},
{q:"Who delivered the little city, and what happened to him afterward?", options:["A poor wise man delivered it by wisdom, yet no man remembered him","A mighty captain, and he was crowned","The king's son, and he was honoured","A prophet, and he was feared"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:15",verse:"Now there was found in it a poor wise man, and he by his wisdom delivered the city; yet no man remembered that same poor man.", insight:"Wisdom saved the city and the wise man was forgotten; do good for God's sake, not for remembrance. He remembers."},
{q:"What is better than strength, yet the poor man's wisdom is despised?", options:["Wisdom","Wealth","Speed","Numbers"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:16",verse:"Then said I, Wisdom is better than strength: nevertheless the poor man’s wisdom is despised, and his words are not heard.", insight:"Wisdom beats strength, but the world despises wisdom in poor clothes; value counsel for its truth, not its speaker's status."},
{q:"Whose words are heard in quiet more than the cry of him that rules among fools?", options:["The words of wise men","The words of kings","The songs of singers","The shouts of soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:17",verse:"The words of wise men are heard in quiet more than the cry of him that ruleth among fools.", insight:"Quiet wisdom outlasts loud folly; you do not need to shout to be heard by those who matter."},
{q:"What is better than weapons of war, and what destroys much good?", options:["Wisdom is better; one sinner destroyeth much good","Gold is better; one thief destroyeth much good","Speed is better; one coward destroyeth much good","Numbers are better; one traitor destroyeth much good"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 9:18",verse:"Wisdom is better than weapons of war: but one sinner destroyeth much good.", insight:"Wisdom outmatches weapons, yet one sinner can undo much good; guard your influence, for folly spreads faster than wisdom."}
]},
{n:10, name:"A Little Folly",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:1",verse:"Dead flies cause the ointment of the apothecary to send forth a stinking savour: so doth a little folly him that is in reputation for wisdom and honour."},
 facts:["Did you know the chapter opens with dead flies spoiling the apothecary's ointment? In his world ointment was precious and carefully compounded, yet a little folly ruins a reputation for wisdom and honour the same way (Ecclesiastes 10:1).","Did you know the Preacher warns that even unspoken curses travel? 'Curse not the king, no not in thy thought... for a bird of the air shall carry the voice' (Ecclesiastes 10:20). Whispered words have wings.","Did you know the Preacher pictures a land blessed when its rulers eat 'in due season, for strength, and not for drunkenness'? Leadership feasts to serve, not to indulge (Ecclesiastes 10:17)."],
 questions:[
{q:"What causes the apothecary's ointment to send forth a stinking savour?", options:["Dead flies","Stale herbs","Dirty vessels","Old oil"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:1",verse:"Dead flies cause the ointment of the apothecary to send forth a stinking savour: so doth a little folly him that is in reputation for wisdom and honour.", insight:"Tiny dead flies spoil precious ointment; small, tolerated sins can stink up a lifetime of honour. Sweep out the little follies."},
{q:"Where is the wise man's heart, and where the fool's?", options:["The wise man's heart is at his right hand; the fool's at his left","Both at the right hand","The wise man's at his left; the fool's at his right","Neither has a heart"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:2",verse:"A wise man’s heart is at his right hand; but a fool’s heart at his left.", insight:"The right hand is the hand of strength and skill; the wise lean toward what is right and able. Aim your heart at strength, not weakness."},
{q:"What does the fool say to everyone as he walks by the way?", options:["That he is a fool","That he is wise","Nothing at all","That the way is long"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:3",verse:"Yea also, when he that is a fool walketh by the way, his wisdom faileth him, and he saith to every one that he is a fool.", insight:"Folly advertises itself; a fool's walk and talk betray him to all. Let your life announce wisdom instead."},
{q:"If the spirit of the ruler rises up against you, what should you do?", options:["Leave not thy place","Flee to another land","Answer him sharply","Gather friends to defend you"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:4",verse:"If the spirit of the ruler rise up against thee, leave not thy place; for yielding pacifieth great offences.", insight:"When authority is angry, do not storm off; staying calm in your place can pacify great offences. Stillness is strength."},
{q:"What evil did the Preacher see under the sun, as an error from the ruler?", options:["Folly set in great dignity, and the rich sitting in low place","War against neighbours","Heavy taxes on the poor","The closing of the gates"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:5",verse:"There is an evil which I have seen under the sun, as an error which proceedeth from the ruler:", insight:"When rulers promote folly and demote worth, the whole land feels it; pray for leaders who honour wisdom."},
{q:"Who sits in low place while folly is set in great dignity?", options:["The rich","The wise","The poor","The young"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:6",verse:"Folly is set in great dignity, and the rich sit in low place.", insight:"The world often seats folly high and worth low; do not measure yourself by man's seating chart. God sees the true order."},
{q:"What upside down sight did the Preacher see?", options:["Servants upon horses, and princes walking as servants upon the earth","Kings ploughing fields","Priests begging bread","Children ruling cities"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:7",verse:"I have seen servants upon horses, and princes walking as servants upon the earth.", insight:"Servants riding while princes walk: the Preacher grieves disordered ranks. Honour diligence, and do not envy the unworthy exalted."},
{q:"What happens to him that digs a pit, and to him that breaks a hedge?", options:["He shall fall into the pit, and a serpent shall bite the hedge breaker","He shall find treasure, and gather honey","He shall be praised, and rewarded","He shall grow rich, and be honoured"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:8",verse:"He that diggeth a pit shall fall into it; and whoso breaketh an hedge, a serpent shall bite him.", insight:"The trapper falls into his own trap, and the boundary breaker meets the serpent; schemes and shortcuts recoil. Walk straight."},
{q:"What happens to whoever removes stones, and to him that cleaves wood?", options:["He shall be hurt by the stones, and endangered by the wood","He shall build a house","He shall be honoured","He shall find rest"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:9",verse:"Whoso removeth stones shall be hurt therewith; and he that cleaveth wood shall be endangered thereby.", insight:"Even honest labour carries risk; stones shift and axes slip. Work carefully, and commit your safety to God."},
{q:"If the iron is blunt and he does not whet the edge, what must he do?", options:["Put to more strength","Buy a new axe","Hire another worker","Rest until morrow"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:10",verse:"If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct.", insight:"A dull axe demands brute strength; sharpening saves sweat. Prepare well, and your work will cost you less."},
{q:"What is the babbler no better than?", options:["The serpent that bites without enchantment","The dog that barks at night","The wind that howls","The fool that sleeps"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:11",verse:"Surely the serpent will bite without enchantment; and a babbler is no better.", insight:"A charmer who cannot charm his serpent is useless, and so is a talker who cannot rule his tongue; let your words be few and true."},
{q:"What are the words of a wise man's mouth, and what do a fool's lips do?", options:["Gracious, while the fool's lips swallow up himself","Many, while the fool's lips win him friends","Loud, while the fool's lips keep him safe","Secret, while the fool's lips make him rich"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:12",verse:"The words of a wise man’s mouth are gracious; but the lips of a fool will swallow up himself.", insight:"Gracious words mark the wise; a fool's own lips devour him. Speak grace, and bridle your tongue before it bridles you."},
{q:"What are the beginning and the end of the fool's talk?", options:["Foolishness at the beginning, and mischievous madness at the end","Wisdom at the beginning, folly at the end","Silence at the beginning, shouting at the end","Truth at the beginning, lies at the end"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:13",verse:"The beginning of the words of his mouth is foolishness: and the end of his talk is mischievous madness.", insight:"Foolish talk starts silly and ends dangerous; watch where words are heading, and stop the ones headed for madness."},
{q:"What can no man tell, though the fool is full of words?", options:["What shall be, and what shall be after him","The name of the king","The number of the stars","The way to the city"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:14",verse:"A fool also is full of words: a man cannot tell what shall be; and what shall be after him, who can tell him?", insight:"The fool talks much about a future he cannot know; the wise speak less and trust more. Leave tomorrow to God."},
{q:"What wearies everyone about the labour of the foolish?", options:["He knoweth not how to go to the city","He works too hard","He sings while he works","He wakes too early"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:15",verse:"The labour of the foolish wearieth every one of them, because he knoweth not how to go to the city.", insight:"The fool cannot find his way to the city, yet labours on; effort without direction exhausts everyone. Learn the way before you run."},
{q:"When is there woe to the land?", options:["When its king is a child, and its princes eat in the morning","When its fields are empty","When its rivers run dry","When its walls fall"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:16",verse:"Woe to thee, O land, when thy king is a child, and thy princes eat in the morning!", insight:"Childish rulers who feast in the morning bring woe; maturity in leadership is a blessing to a whole land. Pray for grown leaders."},
{q:"When is the land blessed?", options:["When its king is the son of nobles, and its princes eat in due season, for strength and not for drunkenness","When its barns are full","When its armies are strong","When its merchants prosper"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:17",verse:"Blessed art thou, O land, when thy king is the son of nobles, and thy princes eat in due season, for strength, and not for drunkenness!", insight:"Noble rulers eat in due season for strength, not indulgence; leadership that serves itself last blesses the whole land."},
{q:"What happens by much slothfulness and idleness of the hands?", options:["The building decays, and the house drops through","The fields flourish","The workers grow strong","The king is pleased"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:18",verse:"By much slothfulness the building decayeth; and through idleness of the hands the house droppeth through.", insight:"Sloth rots the roof over your own head; neglected work collapses on the neglecter. Keep your hands busy and your house sound."},
{q:"For what is a feast made, and what answers all things?", options:["A feast is made for laughter, and money answereth all things","A feast is made for kings, and wisdom answereth all","A feast is made for weddings, and strength answereth all","A feast is made for harvest, and rain answereth all"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:19",verse:"A feast is made for laughter, and wine maketh merry: but money answereth all things.", insight:"Feasts bring laughter and wine brings merriment, but money answers all in the world's economy; do not let it become your master."},
{q:"Why should you not curse the king, not even in your thought?", options:["For a bird of the air shall carry the voice, and that which hath wings shall tell the matter","For the walls have ears of brass","For the servants always listen","For the king reads minds"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 10:20",verse:"Curse not the king, no not in thy thought; and curse not the rich in thy bedchamber: for a bird of the air shall carry the voice, and that which hath wings shall tell the matter.", insight:"Even unspoken curses travel on wings; guard your thoughts about rulers, for words find ways of flying. Honour authority in heart and speech."}
]}
]);
